      "motif": { "channel": "pulse1", "patterns": [2, 4, 11], "variation": "one-row displacement plus a Gxx sub-row nudge in A' (p1:4/p1:5), re-cast as a canon subject in consonant leaps in B (p1:6-p1:9), and reharmonised over the A7-D7 chain with the global peak in A'' (p1:11)" },
      "bank": { "instruments": ["lead-bright", "lead-round", "harm-soft", "echo-thin", "echo-round", "bass", "bass-short", "kick", "snare", "tom", "hat-closed", "hat-open", "crash"], "rev": 1 },
      "percussionGap": 8,
      "notes": "FRACTIONAL TEMPO: T=140 S=6 E=60 gives num=1800 den=280, so the Bresenham row accumulator runs a SEVEN-row cycle of 7 6 7 6 7 6 6 ticks (45 ticks / 7 rows = 6.4286) and returns to zero every 7 rows. The doc's illustrative eight-entry list is not what this engine produces; the seven-row cycle is measured from RowAccumulator and is the lope. Because 7 does not divide 16, the lilt itself drifts against the bar - the swing is already a polymeter before a note is written. POLYRHYTHM (structural, on top of that): the A and A' hat lane is a THREE-ROW cell (hats on every r%3==0 row in A, displaced to r%3==1 in A', noise:2 vs noise:4) crossing the bar under a 4/4 kick and backbeat; B replaces it with a SIX-ROW triangle ostinato (root-fifth-octave every 6 rows, tri:5-tri:8) against 16-row bars, realigning only every 3 bars. DROPPED BEAT: frame 13 rows 25-31, kit out for seven rows after the bar-2 kick (noise:7). METRIC EVENT: frame 13's last row carries D52, so frame 14 is entered at row 52 and contributes a THREE-BEAT pickup bar before A'' lands on a downbeat - Dxx used exactly as section 2.9 describes (a pickup bar without a short pattern), but placed on the frame BEFORE the pickup rather than after it, because entering frame 15 at row 12 would have displaced every downbeat in A''; frame 14 rows 0-51 are written out and simply not played. ECHO vs CANON: A (frames 2-5) is the echo trick proper - pulse 2 plays pulse 1's line THREE ROWS later on echo-thin at about 60% level with P7e (param 126, two raw period units flat); A' frames 6-7 move the echo an octave down onto echo-round; and at frames 8-9 the echo STOPS and pulse 2 becomes a real countermelody on harm-soft with P80 (param 128) cancelling the detune. B (frames 10-13) is a true canon at FOUR rows (one beat) on lead-round at near-equal volume: the subject is written entirely in consonant leaps (thirds, fourths, fifths, sixths - never a second, seventh or tritone between adjacent notes) so leader and follower consonate on every strong beat at that offset. Gxx humanisation: the triangle sits ONE TICK behind the kick through A and A' (G01 on every beat-1 and and-of-2 bass note, tri:1-tri:4) and is dead-on in B. HARMONY: A = i-bVI-bIII-V / i-iv-bVI-V with D7 (f sharp) as the secondary dominant; A' bars 5-8 run a chromatic bass descent g-f#-f-eb into F7, which pivots to B flat major for B; B adds D flat major, the bIII chromatic mediant of B flat (frame 11 bar 2), then chains A7 -> D7 -> Gm (frame 12 bars 3-4), and closes on the Eb -> D half cadence (frame 13). A'' turns around through A7 -> D7 into the loop. WRITTEN DISSONANCES (section 9.2 quota): 4-3 CADENTIAL SUSPENSION at frame 8:44 - pulse 2 attacks g4 (67) as the sixth over B flat, holds it across the barline into the D7 bar where it is the fourth, and resolves down by step to f sharp 4 (66), the third, at 8:52; APPOGGIATURA at frame 8:28 - pulse 2 attacks f4 (65) on a strong beat over E flat and resolves down to e flat 4 (63) two rows later at 8:30. Global peak a5 at frame 17 row 24. DECIMAL PARAMS: P param 126 = P7e = 2 units flat, 128 = P80 = in tune; 4 param 66 = 442 = medium vibrato, 0 = 400 cancel; G param 1/2 = 1- and 2-tick nudges; A param 3 = A03 swell UP (inverted), 0 = A00 stop; D param 52. The suite-wide rmsRange convention: the measured two-pass mix RMS +-1.5 dB, declared per song because the design's [-20,-9] window is unreachable on this engine and the floor is -30 dBFS. Measured -20.53 dBFS, declared [-22.03, -19.03]. Accidentals 8.5%."
